# (thresh)Hold The Line — advances in VTSearch calibration.
# Mildly technical audience with no prior exposure to VTSearch: the opening
# third builds the tool, and lands on the one object the whole talk is about —
# a line through a ranking that decides twice, what comes back and what you are
# asked next. That first third is deliberately click-by-click — make a
# detector, answer questions, run it over media nobody voted on — because a
# room that has not seen the tool cannot be told about a threshold inside it.
#
# Then the ideas, in order: four iterations on where the line goes, then the
# same machinery walked a second time along the Inclusion axis — where
# the *user* wants it — and then the region-voting idea, whose measured
# verdict waits for the Results section with the runs still reporting.
#
# The deck is ideas, not experiments: it presents findings, and the studies
# behind them live in the reports the presenter notes cite (see #3246, #3265).
#
# The outline fragment appears six times: once bare, as the opening list, and
# then once before each section marked with `+at1` … `+at5`. It is one slide
# shown six ways rather than six slides — `build.py` numbers it 1a…1f — and the
# last showing, with "The End" marked, is the deck's closing slide.

theme: vtsearch

slides:
  title-hold-the-line
  outline-hold-the-line

  # 1 — VTSearch
  outline-hold-the-line +at1
  problem
  make-detector
  train-loop
  find-results
  find-line
  embed-flow
  vote-boundary

  # 2 — Calibration
  outline-hold-the-line +at2
  xcal
  gmm-idea
  gmm-em
  blend
  blend-schedule
  anchored-gmm
  anchored-em
  quantile-transfer
  split-fraction

  # 3 — Preference
  outline-hold-the-line +at3
  inclusion-rank
  inclusion-cost
  inclusion-knob
  inclusion-walk
  inclusion-tilt
  inclusion-acq

  # 4 — Regions
  outline-hold-the-line +at4
  voting-modes
  evt-midpoint
  evt-gumbel

  # 5 — The End
  outline-hold-the-line +at5

  # Appendix — after the end, for questions. The Train/Check result is a
  # results slide, not an idea: the argument carries the change, this carries
  # the sweep that justified it, and it comes up only if someone asks.
  split-fraction-result

  # Appendix — the Coverage Atlas: how autopilot decides what to ask when the
  # answer is not "whatever is nearest the line". Parked after the end for now
  # because section 1 is deliberately three beats to the threshold and this is
  # a fourth; the argument it belongs in is *Pics on a Plane*'s last page, which
  # already says the threshold decides what gets asked, and the honest reading
  # of these four is "and here is the half of that the threshold does not do".
  # Move them in front of section 2 when section 1 can afford them.
  #
  # The order is the argument, not the code: the blind spot the atlas exists
  # for, the structure, the second job that structure also does, and how badly
  # it does it. That last one stays last whatever happens to the rest — it is
  # the only slide here that reports a loss, and a loss read before the
  # mechanism is just a reason not to listen.
  atlas-blindspot
  atlas-cells
  atlas-depth
  atlas-pvalues

  # Appendix — the data. Slides that come *before* any experimental result
  # that used them, and sit here only because the deck does not show those
  # results yet (#3958). Move them in front of the results section when it
  # arrives rather than leaving them parked.
  #
  # Every dataset arrives on one card, "Data, Set" (`make-data-cards.py`):
  # the sets somebody else made, what they do not give you ("Not Yet a …"),
  # then what we built from them, in the same card. Twice: COCO into
  # `coco_quarry`, and four document sources into DocMarks.
  #
  # The logo slide sits between the two because it introduces the idea of
  # logo detection before the room is asked to find logos on documents. It is
  # the `book` slide's claim made a second time, in the one domain where a
  # room expects a concept to have a published spec sheet: eight results for
  # one query, arriving one at a time, and the presenter asks about each
  # without answering.
  #
  # The figures are generated from the config the pile builds against and from
  # the DocMarks datasheet, so a rebuilt dataset is a re-run of the figure
  # scripts rather than an edit here — but the presenter notes carry numbers
  # too, and those are hand-written.
  data-set-coco
  data-coco-quarry-complement
  data-coco-problems
  data-set-quarry
  data-coco-quarry-bands
  data-coco-quarry-cells
  logo-grid
  data-set-docmarks-sources
  data-docmarks-problems
  data-set-docmarks
  data-docmarks-shape
